                                  INTRODUCTION
                               ß ßßßßßßßßßßßßßß ß

   Why?
   ~~~~

   Why?  Well take a look around and see if YOU can find any decent Adlib
   trackers around!

   Being a demo group, Reality had occasion to need Adlib tunes for our
 ú intros.  Void (our musician) went to work producing a tune in
 . Scream-Tracker 3, despite the fact that it was designed more as a sample
 ù tracker and Adlib seemed to be a tacked on after-thought.  But that
 : wasn't the main problem.  When it came time to put the tune into the
 | intro....where the hell do you find player code?!
 ³ That's right.  Future Crew didn't see fit to include player code with ST3  ú
   hence making it useless for demo/intro development.                        .
 ³ The technical documentation wasn't thorough enough either so my first      :
 ³ attempt at writing player code for it went miserably awry.                 |
 ³                                                                            ³
 ³ Then we heard that a few groups were using a package called HSC-Tracker    :
 ³ in their intros.  We saw it on the boards so we downloaded it, noting      ³
   that it was described as being "The Ultimate Adlib Tracker" including      ³
 ³ player code.  Well this sounded like our problems were over so we gave it  ³
 | to our musician to try....  Hmmm.  I think I heard him scream from across  ³
 : the Tasman!  To the average user, HSC-Tracker looks like quite a complete  ³
 . package but the actual editor program was too simplistic and unfriendly    ³
 ú for use (sorry Chicken!).  What's more, the included "linkable" player     ³
   code was in OBJect format which doesn't make customising it very easy,     ³
   and is unusable if you use a different MODEL or want to use it in          ³
   protected mode.                                                            ³
                                                                              |
   So after that, the others in the group said "Fuck it.  We'll just have     :
   to use a sample module (not that it's easy finding decent player code for  ù
   that!) and forget about Adlib."  But instead I decided to stop work on     .
 ú the intro and knock up a quick Adlib editor for our musician to use.
 .                                                                            ú
 ù Hence was born RAD, which stands for Reality ADlib tracker.  In the end,
 : we got so involved in putting everything WE wanted into RAD that it has
 | had a lot more effort put into it than was originally intended.  It is
 ³ now a fully-fledged tracker, and we think it is the best Adlib tracker
 ³ you're likely to find in the scene.  But not just that, for the demo
 ³ crews out there, RAD comes complete with full player source-code for
 º playing RAD tunes in your intros/demos.                                    .
 ³ Just like the old days on Amiga...! :)                                     :
 º                                                                            ³
 ÌÍÄÍÍÄÍÄÄÄ ÄÄ Ä - ù ú              Ä ÄÄ ÄÄÄ- ÄÄÄÄÄÍÄÄÍÄÍÍÄÍÍÍÄÄÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ´
 ³                                                                            |
 ³ What Does It Do?                                                           :
 ³ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                           ù
 ³
 | RAD, as with all trackers, is a program that allows you to author musical
 : arrangements.  It was based more-or-less on Protracker (on the Amiga)
 . which is the model that most music trackers seem to be based on.

       Tracker tunes are made up of three main components:
                                                                              ú
                   * Instruments
                   * Order list                                               ù
                   * Patterns                                                 .
                                                                              :
       These will be described later.                                         |
                                                                              ³

       RAD has the following features:                                        ³
                                                                              º
 ú                 * Up to 31 separate instruments                            ³
 ù                 * Up to 32 separate patterns                               º
 :                 * Up to 128 patterns in the order                          º
 ³                 * 9 channels                                               º
 ³                 * Compact tune file size.                                  ³
 º                  (A fraction of the size of OTHER trackers!)               º
 ³                 * Easy to use                                              º
 º                                                                            ³
 ÌÍÍÍÍÍÄÍÍÍÄÍÍÄÍÄÄÄÄÄÄ Ä -ù ú                                     ú ù Ä ÄÄ ÄÄÄ¹
