 ³ Starting RAD                                                               ³
 º ~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                               |
 ³                                                                            :
 ³     To start RAD, run the .EXE file, ie:                                   ù
 |                                                                            ú
 :              RAD<enter>
 .
 ú You will then see the main RAD screen with credits information in the      ú
   lower half.  Press a key to clear the credits window.                      .



 ú Obtaining help while using RAD
 . ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 |
 ³ To enter the multi-page help system, press "?".  Keys handy to know
 ³ while traversing the pages:
 ³
 ³     ESC            - Return to the pattern editor
 ³     ?              - Go back to the last major menu
 ³     BACKSPACE      - Go back to the last page you were on (up to 64 times)
 |     WHITE letters  - Keys to press to move to another page.
 ³
 . As from V1.1a, a new quick-key reference has been added.  Press F12 and    ú
 | you will see the start of a large scrollable document where the Pattern    ù
 : Editor used to be, which gives a run down on all the keys you can use      .
 . within RAD.                                                                :
 ú                                                                            |
                                                                              ³
                                                                              ³
   Layout of RAD                                                              |
   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                              :
                                                                              .
   As tracker tunes have three main parts, RAD's screen is split up into      ú
   three areas corresponding to those parts.  In the top half of the screen
 ú in the left-hand window is the Instrument Editor.  To the right of this
 ù window is another, thinner window that is the Order-List Editor.
 . The entire lower half of the screen is (as in most trackers) the Pattern
 : Editor.  The first key to know is the " ` " (back tick) key.  This
 | Switches between the three areas of the screen as you can only be using
 ³ one area at a time.
 ³                                                                            ú
 ³ When you first enter RAD, you start off in the Pattern Editor.             .
 :                                                                            :
 ÃÄÄÄÄÄÄ ÄÄ Ä- - ù ú                                                 ú -Ä ÄÄÄÄ´
 |                                                                            |
 : Pattern Editor                                                             :
 . ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                             ú
 ú                                                                            .
   The Pattern Editor is where you enter the actual notes that will be        ù
   played.  RAD allows up to 32 separate patterns and each pattern is made    :
   up of 64 lines.  Each line has 9 entries corresponding to the 9 Adlib      ³
   channels.  As RAD plays a pattern it steps through each line at the given  .
   speed (described later) outputting the note in each column (if there IS    ³
   one) to the corresponding Adlib channel.  Each entry in the pattern is     |
   made up of FOUR items, eg.                                                 ³
                                                                              ³
       C#3 2C30                                                               º
       ÃÙ³ ³³ÃÙ                                                               ³
       ÀÄÅÄÅÅÅÄÄÄÄÄÄ 1) The NOTE that is played. "#" = sharp, "-" = normal.   º
         ³ ³³³                                                                º
         ÀÄÅÅÅÄÄÄÄÄÄ 2) The OCTAVE of the note. From 0 to 7.                  º
           ³³³                                                                º
           ÀÅÅÄÄÄÄÄÄ 3) The INSTRUMENT the note is played in (in Hex).        ³
 ú          ³³                                                                º
 .          ÀÅÄÄÄÄÄÄ 4) The SPECIAL EFFECT, (if any) to perform.              º
 :           ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ and the parameter for the special effect.             ³
 |                                                                            º
 ³                                                                            ³
 ³ The note item follows usual musical notation.  There are twelve available  ³
 | notes per octave:                                                          .
 ù                                                                            ³
 :     C, C#, D, D#, E, F, F#, G, G#, A, A#, B                                |
 ú                                                                            :
   You can write a note in a given position in the pattern by pressing        .
   one of the NOTE KEYS, as set out below:                                    ú

 Actual
   Note-   C#  D#      F#  G#  A#      C#  D#      F#  G#  A#      C#  D#
