   Keys-ŞÛÛ SŞÛ DŞÛÛŞÛÛ GŞÛ HŞÛ JŞÛÛŞÛÛ 2ŞÛ 3ŞÛÛŞÛÛ 5ŞÛ 6ŞÛ 7ŞÛÛŞÛÛ 9ŞÛ 0ŞÛÛ
         ŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛ
         ŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛŞÛÛ  ŞÛ  ŞÛÛ
         ŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛ
   Keys-ŞİZÛŞİXÛŞİCÛŞİVÛŞİBÛŞİNÛŞİMÛŞİQÛŞİWÛŞİEÛŞİRÛŞİTÛŞİYÛŞİUÛŞİIÛŞİOÛŞİPÛ
         ŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛŞÛÛÛ
 Actual
   Note-  C   D   E   F   G   A   B   C   D   E   F   G   A   B   C   D   E


   When you write a note, the octave is also written.  You can change the
   current octave using the "," and "." keys.  The currently selected
   instrument is also written and you will hear the instrument played at
   the given pitch.
 ú
 . You can change instruments by pressing "[" and "]".
 :                                                                            ú
 | You can navigate the Pattern Editor by using the CURSOR KEYS,              ù
 ³ and the following:                                                         .
 ³                                                                            :
 ù     TAB     - move to the next channel                                     |
 :     BackTAB - move to the previous channel                                 ³
 .     PgUp    - move up 16 lines                                             ³
 ú     PgDown  - move down 16 lines                                           ³
       Home    - move to the top of the pattern                               :
       End     - move to the end of the pattern                               ³
                                                                              |
   To add/alter special effects, cursor over to the special effects           :
   column and use one of the following numbers/letters:                       .
                                                                              ú
       0       - No effect

       1       - Slide note down (pitch bend).  Parameter gives speed of
                 slide.

 ú     2       - Slide note up (pitch bend).  Parameter gives speed of slide.
 ù
 .     3       - Slide to note.  This slides the LAST note played till it
 :               reaches the note given with this effect.  The parameter
 |               gives the speed of the slide.  Use parameter "00" in
 ³               subsequent lines to continue the slide.
 :
 ³     5       - Combined slide to note & Volume slide.  This command has
 ³               the same functionality as effect 3 with parameter "00" but
 ù               in this case, the parameter details a volume slide.
 ³               1-49 fades the volume of the instrument down.
 ³               51-99 fades the volume of the instrument up.
 º
 ³     A       - Volume slide.  1-49 fades the volume of the instrument down
 º               at the given speed.  51-99 fades the volume of the
 º               instrument up at the given speed.
 ³
 º     C       - Set volume.  Sets the absolute volume of the instrument.
 º               Values range from 0 (silent) to 64 (loudest).
 º
 º     D       - Break.  When RAD encounters this command it skips the rest
 º               of the pattern and moves to the next pattern as specified
 º               in the Order List.  The parameter, if given, specifies a
 º               line in the next pattern to start from.
 º               eg.  Effect "D12" would break to line 12 of the next pattern
 º                    in the Order List.
 ³               This command is handy for "cutting short" a pattern if
 º               you don't want to use all 64 lines.  Just place a D00 on the
 º               last line you used and the rest will be ignored.
 ³
 º     F       - Set speed.  By default RAD starts up with a speed of 6 but
 ³               you can use this command to slow down and speed up the
 ³               playback rate of your tune.  To understand the speed value,
 |               know that RAD uses a 50Hz timer which is delayed by the
 ³               speed value between each line of the pattern.                ú
 |               What this means is that, say, if you set the speed to 50,
 :               (using "F50"), then each line will play at the rate of one   ú
 ù               per second.  Special effects, however, are modified          ù
 ú               50 times a second.                                           .
                                                                              :
   To play the current pattern (to see how it sounds) press F6.               |
   Pressing F6 again will stop the playback.  To move to a new pattern        ³
   press "-" and "+/=".                                                       .
                                                                              ³
   You can use the following editing keys to help you in writing patterns:    ³
                                                                              ³
       INSERT  - insert a line in the current channel                         º
       DELETE  - delete the current line in the current channel               ³
       SPACEBAR- wipe the current item only (also stops playback).            º
 ú     F1      - Move to the top left corner of the region you wish to copy   º
 .               and press F1.  Then move to the lower right corner and       º
 :               press F1 again.  The highlighted region will then be moved   ³
 |               into a copy buffer.                                          º
 :     F2      - Will paste the contents of the copy buffer to the current    ³
 ³               cursor location.                                             |
 .     F3      - Will copy the entire pattern into the copy buffer.           :
 ³     F4      - Will replace the entire current pattern with the contents    .
 ³               of the copy buffer (be careful!).
 ³     F9      - decrement the current speed value                            ú
 ³     F10     - increment the current speed value                            :
 ³                                                                            |
 ÆÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÄÍÍÍÄÍÍÄÍÄÄÍÄÄ ÄÄÄ ÄÄ Ä- Ä - ù ú                 ú - Ä -Ä ÄÄ ÄÄÄÄµ
 |                                                                            ³
 : Instrument Editor                                                          ³
 . ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                          ³
 ú                                                                            ³
   To edit your instruments you need to switch to the Instrument Editor.      ³
   Use the " ` " (back tick) key to switch between windows until you see a    ³
   highlight bar appear over the top-left item in the instrument window.      |
                                                                              :
   RAD supports up to 31 instruments and it is these instruments that         ù
   actually produce the sound.  Each note in your patterns specifies an       ú
   instrument that that note is played on.  Instruments can vary
   significantly in sound from each other and, accordingly, your tune's
   mood will change dramatically depending on the instrument mix you use.
   Some instruments sound good as lead, other as bass and others are only
   useful as percussion.

   To begin with you will probably find it easier just to load
   pre-written tunes and use the instruments that came with them (you can
   do this by loading the tune and pressing Control-C to clear the patterns
   leaving the instruments intact).

   However, to design your own instruments, once you are in the Instrument
 ú Editor you can interactively alter the currently selected instrument's
 . properties (remember "[" and "]" select instruments).  Use Cursor Up and
 ù Down to select the property to alter, and Cursor Left and Right to
 : decrease or increase or toggle the property's value.  Alternatively,
 . you can press ENTER on the parameter you wish to change, and input the
   value manually with the numeric keys.  There isn't much to say regarding
 : how to correctly set properties, as different things produce different
 | effects.  Simply alter things and press a Note Key to see how it sounds!
 ³ The one property's effect you SHOULD be aware of is the CARRIER's volume.
 . This governs the output volume of the instrument and, in fact, this is
 ³ the property that is modified by the Set Volume and Volume Slide commands.
 º
 ³ Other than that,  Good Luck! :-)
 º
 º If you create a cool sounding instrument, you'll probably want to put
 º it aside somewhere for future use.  Use Control-G (Get instrument) and
 ³ Control-P (Put instrument) to load and save instruments.  Refer to         .
 º Loading and Saving below on how to use the interactive directory.          :
 ³                                                                            |
 ÃÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ ÄÄ Ä- Ä - ú                         Ä ÄÄ ÄÄÄ ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ¹
 :                                                                            º
 . Order List Editor                                                          º
 ú ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                          º
                                                                              º
   Once you have created a few patterns you will want to string them          ³
   together to create a complete tune.  By moving to the Order Editor (use    º
   " ` " remember) you can define when each pattern will be played.  Line 00  º
   corresponds to the first pattern to be played, line 01 the second, etc.    ³
   By using Cursor Up & Down to select a line, with Insert or Delete to       º
   remove or add a line as necessary, you can use Cursor Left and Right to    ³
   set which pattern will be played at that position in the song.             ³
   for example, suppose you had the following Order List:                     :
                                                                              ³
                 00³03                                                        .
                 01³01                                                        :
 ú               02³04                                                        ù
 .               03³01                                                        ú
 :               04³02
 |               0502
 ³
 : When you start your tune playing (with F5), it will start by playing
 ³ pattern 03 (specifed by line 00).  Once it has played all 64 lines of
 º pattern 03 (or less if you used a Break command) it will then play the
 ³ pattern specified by the next line, in this case, pattern 01.
 º Each line will be played one after the other, playing the pattern listed
 º for that line.
 º As you can see in the above example you can re-use patterns (Pattern 01
 ³ is used in line 01 and 03) if there is a chorus or refrain you want to
 º repeat in your tune.
 ³
 | If you notice in the above example, there is a line that contains a
 ³ right arrow between the line and the pattern.  This is a JUMP MARKER
 : and it tells RAD to jump to a new line.  So when RAD gets to line 05 in    .
 . the example, rather than playing pattern 02 it will instead jump to LINE   :
 ú 02.  Hence the list of patterns that will be played with the above Order   |
   List is:                                                                   ³
                                                                              ³
                 03, 01, 04, 01, 02, 04, 01, 02, 04, 01, 02....               ³
                                      ³                                       ³
                                      ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄ Repeat caused by Jump Marker    ³
                                                                              ³
   You can mark the current line as a jump marker by pressing '\' and         º
   then using Cursor Left & Right to set the line the marker will jump to.    ³
   A Jump Marker cannot be placed on Line 00.                                 º
                                                                              º
 ú RAD will repeat the song from Line 00 when it gets to the end of the       ³
 . Order List so you don't need to place a Jump Marker that jumps to Line     º
 : 00 at the end of your Order List if you want your tune to repeat.          º
 |                                                                            º
 ÃÄÄ Ä- Ä ù ú                                            ú ù Ä ÄÄ ÄÄÄÄÄÍÄÍÍÄÍÍ¹
 ³                                                                            ³
 ³ Loading & Saving Tunes                                                     º
 : ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                                                     ³
 ³                                                                            ³
 | Naturally enough, once you have completed your Symphony No. 5, you'll      ³
 : want to keep a permanent record of it.  To save your tune press            ³
 ù Control-S to bring up the directory.  You can select an existing           :
 ú filename by positioning the highlight bar on it, using the cursor keys,    ³
   and then pressing Enter.  By selecting a directory or a device you can     |
   move around your disk structure.  If you are saving a new file then you    :
   will need to type a new filename.  Press TAB to switch to the filename     ù
   field and enter the desired filename, using the editing keys to fix        ú
   typing mistakes as necessary.  Press Enter to save.  If you are saving
   over an existing file you will be asked to confirm.

   Loading follows a similar procedure.  Press Control-L and use the
   directory and filename field to select the desired tune file.  Whereas
 ú saving, however, only creates RAD compatible files, you may load not
 ù only RAD files, but ScreamTracker 3 and HSC-Tracker compatible files.
 .
 : Note:
 |      Due to differences in structure between the different tracker
 ³      packages, converted tunes will sometimes not sound like their
 ³      originals.  Therefore loading .S3M and .HSC tunes should really be
 ³      viewed as a tool for converting these formats to RAD format, rather
 :      than for general purpose playing.
 ³
 |
 : Adding a Description
 . ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 ú
   Because tracker tunes circulate widely and are often not accompanied
   by documentation, traditionally musicians have placed author information
   and greets in the instrument fields of their compositions.  RAD does not
   have instrument fields but you can add a description to your tune by
   entering the Description Editor.  Press Control-D to toggle between
   editing the Description and editing the Pattern.  The Description Editor
   is a simple free-form one page text editor.  Type whatever you like,
   where you like, even if it's just the name of the tune!
                                                                              ú
   Because RAD was written as an intro tune development system, the tune      ù
   files were designed to be fairly compact so as not to take up much room    :
 ú in any programs it was used in.  Adding a Description to a tune will       |
 . add to the length of the tune.  In the bottom right corner of the          .
 : Description Editor is a counter that displays the number of bytes that     ³
 ³ the current Description text will occupy in the saved file so you'll       .
 . always know just how much space your hot air is going to take up! :-)      ³
 ³                                                                            ³
 ³ You may not see much use for a Description especially if the tune is       ³
 º ultimately destined for use in an intro but the fact is module-ripping     ³
 ³ is a popular past-time and chances are, if your tune is awesome, it WILL   ³
 º be ripped out of your intro and spread around.  You can at least make      |
 º sure everyone knows who wrote the ripped tune because the description      :
 ³ will be ripped along with the tune!                                        ù
 º                                                                            ú
 ÌÍÄÍÄÄÍÍÄÄÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÄÍÍÍÄÍÍÄÄÍÄÄÄÄ ÄÄ Ä- Ä ù ú
 º
 ³                                                                            ú
 º                               CODING NOTES                                 ù
 ³                            ß ßßßßßßßßßßßßßß ß                              :
 ³                                                                            |
 ³ Yo coding-type dude!                                                       ³
 ³ This section is geared more towards the seasoned coder, so skip this if    ³
 ³ you're not!                                                                ³
 :                                                                            ³
 ³ RAD's whole reason for being, is as a tool for writing tunes for use       ³
 | in intros.  To this end, the FULL player source-code for playing RAD       ³
 : tunes in your productions is included.  The format follows that            |
 ù traditionally set by trackers on the Amiga.  Three routines are            :
   provided:                                                                  .
                                                                              ú
       InitPlayer
       EndPlayer
       PlayMusic

   During your initialisation step in your intro, include a call to
   "InitPlayer" with ES:0 pointing to a RAD module.  When you close down
   your intro or want the tune to stop, call "EndPlayer".  Now to have the
   tune play, you need to call PlayMusic 50 times a second for a tune
   written in "normal timer mode" or 18.2 times a second (the standard PC
   timer cycle) for a tune written in "slow timer mode".  You can check bit
 ú 6 of the byte at offset 17 in the RAD module to see which timer mode the
 ù tune was written for.  If the bit is set then it is a "slow timer" tune.
 :
 | The "slow timer mode" was provided for those people that just want a
 ³ simple interrupt structure for playing the music, as the normal 50Hz rate
 : requires a re-program of the hardware clock which may be needed as a
 ³ Vertical Refresh timer.  As a lot of intros provide support for calling
 ³ the system interrupt 18.2 times a second it is easy enough to add a call
 º to PlayMusic there.  If you don't then you'll have to use one of the
 ³ Adlib timers to get a 50Hz or 18.2Hz reference signal for when to call
 º PlayMusic during your Vertical Refresh interrupt.
 º
 ³ By pressing '*' in RAD you toggle normal and slow timer modes for the
 º current tune.  HSC tunes are automatically loaded as slow-timer tunes
 º for compatibility.
 º
 º The provided player code was written as a real-mode player and to
 º reference variables through CS: because I didn't want to use ASSUMEs
 º which would replace any ASSUMEs you had set up in your main code.  Be
 ³ careful if you wish to remove the CS: overrides as the player uses DS:
 º as a pointer to the tune file.  You will HAVE to remove the CS:            ú
 º overrides if you wish to use the player in protected mode.                 ù
 ³                                                                            :
 º But then, that's the beauty of having the full source code to the          ³
 ³ player - you can hack it around all you like to fit in with your code,     .
 | something that OTHER tracker writers don't seem to realise with their      ³
 : .OBJ format players, WHEN they bother to release players for their         ³
 . trackers at all...                                                         ³
 ú                                                                            º
   Oh...  If you DO use RAD or its player in your production, all we ask is   ³
   that you give REALITY a mega-greet or credits somewhere! :-)               º
                                                                              º
   ** NOTE:  Apologies to all who used the V1.0 player code only to find it   ³
             didn't play tunes exactly as they played within RAD.  This bug   º
             in the PLAYER.ASM code has been mashed!                          º
                                                                              ³
   Since the first release of RAD, some coders have asked why I require the   º
   player code to go through the timer interrupt.  What a few people don't    º
   seem to realise is that I've only SUGGESTED this as an example, as this    º
 ú is the most likely method of calling the player code.  For correct play-   º
 ù back of your RAD tune, you SHOULD call PlayMusic 50/s (or 18.2/s) but how  º
 . you supply these rates is entirely up to you!                              º
 |                                                                            º
